window.docContents[213].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___10___-Historical-interlude___-Sophus-Lie___s-trials-and-transformations/Historical-interlude-on-Sophus-Lie-_LPAR_continued-and-conclusion_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___10_continued":{"contents":"and preferred citing his own earlier papers [47, p. 310]. He had even less luck with the choice of assistant to write up results on contact transformations and partial differential equations. Felix Hausdorff's interests led him elsewhere, and Lie's thoughts on these subjects were never completely spelled out [16, p. 324]. Thus we may never discover the \"true Lie\".\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.10. Historical interlude: Sophus Lie's trials and transformations\tHistorical interlude on Sophus Lie (continued and conclusion)","header":"Historical interlude on Sophus Lie (continued and conclusion)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___10___-Historical-interlude___-Sophus-Lie___s-trials-and-transformations/Historical-interlude-on-Sophus-Lie-_LPAR_continued-and-conclusion_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___10_continued"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Chapter3___Remark3___8___6":{"contents":"Remark 3.8.6. Thus, we see that, in general, the Krull-Schmidt theorem fails for infinite dimensional modules. However, it still holds for modules of finite length, i.e., modules M such that any filtration of M has length bounded above by a certain constant l = l(M).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\tKrull-Schmidt for modules of finite length","header":"Krull-Schmidt for modules of finite length","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Krull-Schmidt-for-modules-of-finite-length/#Chapter3___Remark3___8___6"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Section-7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___1___heading-1":{"contents":"We have now seen many examples of representation theories and of operations with representations (direct sum, tensor product, induction, restriction, reflection functors, etc.). A context in which one can systematically talk about this is provided by category theory.\n\nCategory theory was founded by Saunders Mac Lane and Samuel Eilenberg in 1942—1945. It is a fairly abstract theory which seemingly has no content, for which reason it was christened \"abstract nonsense\" (see Section 7.10). Nevertheless, it is a very flexible and powerful language, which has become totally indispensable in many areas of mathematics, such as algebraic geometry, topology, representation theory, and many others.\n\nWe will now give a very short introduction to category theory, highlighting its relevance to the topics in representation theory we have discussed. For a serious acquaintance with category theory, the reader may use, for instance, the classical book [McL].\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.1. The definition of a category\tSection 7.1: The definition of a category","header":"7.1. The definition of a category","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Section-7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___1___heading-1"}});
